* The metacode of Chapter 6 of the Refal-5 manual, exercised end to end.
*
* The metacode table rewrites exactly one symbol -- the asterisk becomes
* *V -- and leaves brackets alone. Dn metacodes its argument, Up restores it,
* and Up *activates* the calls it recovers, because the metacode of the call
* <F e.Args> is the asterisk followed by a bracket holding the function name
* and the metacoded arguments.
*
* A deferred metacode is the asterisk, an exclamation mark, and a bracket: an
* expression already in the form the transformation wants, which Up reproduces
* verbatim instead of lifting.
*
* Section 6.4 adds a fourth kind of view-field object, the *unknown*, which is
* what a free variable's metacode lifts to. An unknown carries a type (S, T or
* E), a level, and an index; Up raises the level and Dn lowers it, and the type
* is taken into account when matching. `Prout` renders one in the form the
* reference gives for the tracer, `#t.ni` -- the marker, the type, a dot, the
* level and the index run together.
*
* In this dialect the asterisk is a one-character symbol, so the manual's
* single-symbol markers are written as two terms here; they print the same.

$EXTERNAL Dn, Prout, Up;

$ENTRY Go {
  = <Prout 'dn ' <Dn 'a*b'>>
    <Prout 'up ' <Up 'a*Vb'>>
    <Prout 'br ' <Up <Dn ('a' ('b') 'c')>>>
    <Prout 'ca ' <Up '*'((Echo) 'Z')>>
    <Prout 'df ' <Up '*!'('*E')>>
    <Prout 'u0 ' <Up '*S' 1>>
    <Prout 'u1 ' <Up <Up '*T' 2>>>
    <Prout 'd0 ' <Dn <Up '*E' 3>>>
    <Prout 'rt ' <Dn <Dn <Up <Up '*S' 4>>>>>
    <Prout 'ms ' <Probe <Up '*S' 5>>>
    <Prout 'mt ' <Probe <Up '*T' 6>>>
    <Prout 'me ' <Probe <Up '*E' 7>>>
    <Prout 'lt ' <Literal <Up '*S' 8>>>;
}

Echo {
  e.X = e.X;
}

* Section 6.4: "the system knows that an s-type unknown denotes some symbol and
* a t-type unknown some term; this is taken into account when matching". So an
* `s.` variable takes an s-unknown, a `t.` variable takes an s- or a t-unknown,
* and an `e.` variable takes all three.
Probe {
  s.X = 's';
  t.Y = 't';
  e.Z = 'e';
}

* A literal does not match an unknown of any kind: an unknown marks a step the
* machine has not decided, so nothing that would decide it may match.
Literal {
  'a' = 'matched';
  e.X = 'not-matched';
}
